---
import CalcPage from '../components/CalcPage.astro';
import def from '../calculators/rafter';
import { rafter, ftIn } from '../lib/framing';
import { pitchFactor } from '../lib/finish';
import { fmt } from '../lib/format';
import { PITCHES } from '../calculators/shingle';

const spans = [12, 16, 20, 24, 28, 32];
const pitchesShown = [4, 6, 8, 12];
---

<CalcPage
  slug="rafter-calculator"
  def={def}
  title="Rafter Calculator: Rafter Length from Span & Pitch"
  description="Free rafter length calculator. Enter the building span, roof pitch, overhang, and ridge board to get the rafter length, rise, plumb cut angle, and number of rafters."
  h1="Rafter Calculator"
  updated="2026-09-26"
>
  <Fragment slot="lead">
    Find the length of common rafters for a gable roof from the building span, pitch, and overhang, plus the rise, the
    plumb cut angle, and how many rafters to buy.
  </Fragment>

  <h2 id="how-to">How to calculate rafter length</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Run = half the building span, minus half the ridge board thickness.</li>
    <li>Multiply the run by the pitch factor, √(1 + (pitch ÷ 12)²), to get the length along the slope.</li>
    <li>Add the overhang, also multiplied by the pitch factor.</li>
    <li>Round up to the next standard lumber length.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2 id="example">Example: 24 ft span, 6/12 pitch, 12 in overhang</h2>
  <p>
    The run is 144 − 0.75 = 143.25 in. At 6/12 the pitch factor is 1.118, so the rafter from ridge to wall is 160.2 in;
    the overhang adds 13.4 in, for 173.6 in, or <strong>14 ft 5⅝ in</strong>. Buy 16 ft boards. The ridge sits 71⅝ in
    above the wall plates.
  </p>

  <h2 id="table">Rafter length by span and pitch</h2>
  <p>With a 12 in level overhang and a 1½ in ridge board.</p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Span</th>{pitchesShown.map((p) => <th class="num">{p}/12</th>)}</tr></thead>
      <tbody>{spans.map((s) => <tr><td>{s} ft</td>{pitchesShown.map((p) => <td class="num">{ftIn(rafter(s, p, 12, 1.5).totalIn)}</td>)}</tr>)}</tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="factors">Pitch factors and angles</h2>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Pitch</th><th class="num">Angle</th><th class="num">Factor</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>{PITCHES.map((p) => <tr><td>{p}/12</td><td class="num">{fmt((Math.atan(p / 12) * 180) / Math.PI, 1)}°</td><td class="num">{fmt(pitchFactor(p), 3)}</td></tr>)}</tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>
    Once you know the rafter length and count, <a href="/board-foot-calculator/">total the lumber in board feet</a>, and
    <a href="/shingle-calculator/">estimate shingle bundles for the same roof</a> from its span and pitch.
  </p>

  <h2 id="faq">Frequently asked questions</h2>
  <h3>What size rafters do I need?</h3>
  <p>
    That depends on the span, spacing, wood species and grade, and the snow and dead loads where you live. The span
    tables in the IRC (R802) list allowed spans for each combination. This calculator gives the length, not the lumber
    size.
  </p>
  <h3>What is the plumb cut angle?</h3>
  <p>
    The angle of the vertical cut at the ridge, equal to the roof angle. Set a speed square or saw to the angle shown, or
    mark it with the pitch numbers on a framing square.
  </p>
  <p>Planning the roofing too? The <a href="/shingle-calculator/">shingle calculator</a> uses the same pitch.</p>

  <h2 id="method">How this calculator works</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Run = span ÷ 2 − ridge ÷ 2; length = run × √(1 + (pitch ÷ 12)²) + overhang × the same factor.</li>
    <li>Rafters per side = building length ÷ spacing + 1, doubled for both slopes.</li>
    <li>Birdsmouth cuts, fascia, and hip or valley rafters are not included.</li>
  </ul>
</CalcPage>
